From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bottlerocket Builder <bottlerocket@amazon.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libblkid: keep only supported partition-table probers

Bottlerocket only ever creates and consumes GPT (and, for the protective
MBR that GPT requires, DOS/pMBR) partition tables. The remaining
partition-table probers are for legacy formats that never appear on
Bottlerocket's cloud block devices.

One of them, the Atari prober, has no magic number and identifies a
partition table purely by loose structural heuristics. On a large,
freshly-encrypted EBS volume, the pseudorandom bytes that back
never-written blocks can coincidentally satisfy those heuristics, so
libblkid reports a phantom "atari" partition table. mkfs.xfs then refuses
to format the BOTTLEROCKET-DATA partition and the node fails to boot
(~1/1800 launches on ~1 TB volumes). See:
  - https://github.com/bottlerocket-os/bottlerocket/issues/4855
  - https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/issues/1116
  - https://github.com/rook/rook/issues/7940

Restrict the partition prober list to the formats Bottlerocket supports
(gpt, dos, and the GPT-protective pmbr). This eliminates the Atari
false positive at the source and reduces the attack surface of libblkid
by dropping unused legacy parsers.

The unused prober .c files are intentionally left in the build (their
externs stay declared in partitions.h); only the registration array is
trimmed. This keeps the patch confined to a single source file so it does
not conflict with Makemodule.am / meson.build on upstream version bumps.

Signed-off-by: Bottlerocket Builder <bottlerocket@amazon.com>
---
 libblkid/src/partitions/partitions.c | 10 ----------
 1 file changed, 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libblkid/src/partitions/partitions.c b/libblkid/src/partitions/partitions.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/libblkid/src/partitions/partitions.c
+++ b/libblkid/src/partitions/partitions.c
@@ -124,19 +124,9 @@
  */
 static const struct blkid_idinfo *idinfos[] =
 {
-	&aix_pt_idinfo,
-	&sgi_pt_idinfo,
-	&sun_pt_idinfo,
 	&dos_pt_idinfo,
 	&gpt_pt_idinfo,
 	&pmbr_pt_idinfo,	/* always after GPT */
-	&mac_pt_idinfo,
-	&ultrix_pt_idinfo,
-	&bsd_pt_idinfo,
-	&unixware_pt_idinfo,
-	&solaris_x86_pt_idinfo,
-	&minix_pt_idinfo,
-	&atari_pt_idinfo
 };

 /*
